kern-tools self-test
====================

Hermetic regression tests for the bundled Kconfiglib (Kconfiglib/kconfiglib.py)
and symbol_why.py. No kernel checkout and no compiler are required to run them.

    ./run-selftest.sh          (or: make selftest)

runs two independent checks and exits non-zero if either fails:

1. Kernel Kconfig parse
   ---------------------
   sync-kernel-kconfig.sh snapshots a real kernel's full Kconfig structure --
   every Kconfig* file, the scripts/*.sh helpers that scripts/Kconfig.include
   shells out to at parse time, and scripts/dummy-tools/ (the kernel's fake
   gcc/ld) -- into kconfig-test-data.tar.gz. run-selftest.sh unpacks that and
   parses the whole thing via "symbol_why.py --selftest", using the dummy tools
   so no real toolchain is needed.

   This is the broad early-warning test: if the kernel introduces a Kconfig
   construct the bundled Kconfiglib can't parse (as v7.0 did with
   "depends on X if Y"), this fails -- whether or not we anticipated it. It
   checks parse success only, not values.

   The snapshot is GENERATED LOCALLY, not committed (it is .gitignore'd, ~2 MB
   gzipped / ~13 MB unpacked -- too large and too churny to track). Generate or
   refresh it with:

       ./sync-kernel-kconfig.sh /path/to/linux-source

   which writes kconfig-test-data.tar.gz + kconfig-test-data.manifest
   (provenance). Until it exists, run-selftest.sh SKIPS this check with a
   reminder. Re-running after a kernel bump tells you, before any image build,
   whether Kconfiglib needs updating for the new kernel.

2. Conditional-dependency semantics
   ---------------------------------
   fixtures/conditional_dep/ is a tiny synthetic Kconfig plus
   test_conditional_dep.py, which loads it through Kconfiglib and asserts
   computed symbol values for "depends on X if Y" -- including the compound
   tristate case where the faithful "X || (cond == n)" encoding differs from a
   naive "!cond". This catches a *semantic* regression that the parse test
   (parse only) would miss. It needs no generated data and always runs.

Notes
-----
- Kconfiglib parses only Kconfig files -- never Makefiles, C source or headers
  -- which is why a Kconfig*-plus-helpers snapshot is complete for parsing.
- The in-build audit (kernel-yocto.bbclass do_kernel_configcheck, and
  tools/kconf_check) already exercises symbol_why.py against the real kernel
  source; this directory is the standalone/CI counterpart that needs no kernel.
